# config/kernel/Kconfig.hardening — Hardening: user allocator, slab, KASLR

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ring-3 allocator (userspace/lib/ualloc)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The knobs of the hardened allocator native ring-3 programs link
# (`azos_ualloc`, Scudo's shape: size-class blocks, randomized chunk
# placement, checksummed chunk headers, a quarantine of freed chunks, guard
# pages). They are plain integers and bools read through `azos_limits`, not
# cargo features: the allocator is a userspace library, not a kernel feature.
# Defaults follow the SECURITY posture: every posture but `general` starts
# with the quarantine, the free-chunk poison and the randomized placement on.

config USER_ALLOC_QUARANTINE_KB
    int "Ring-3 allocator: quarantine of freed small chunks, KiB (0 = off)"
    range 0 4096
    default 64 if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default 0
    help
      Total slot bytes the allocator holds back after a free before a chunk
      can be handed out again. A chunk in quarantine keeps its header in the
      "quarantined" state, so a second free of it is a detected double free,
      and (USER_ALLOC_POISON_FREED) a write through a stale pointer is
      detected when the chunk leaves quarantine. Chunks leave in random
      order when USER_ALLOC_RANDOMIZE is on, oldest first otherwise. 0
      turns the quarantine off: a freed chunk is reusable at once, and a
      double free is detected only until its chunk is allocated again.
      Cost: the bytes are resident while held (Scudo's default is 256 KiB
      per thread cache, this is per process). Large allocations are never
      quarantined: they are unmapped on free and their address range is
      never reused (the kernel's mmap only moves the break up and brk does
      not shrink it; a kernel that lowered the break would void this), so a
      stale pointer into one faults.

config USER_ALLOC_QUARANTINE_SLOTS
    int "Ring-3 allocator: quarantine entries"
    range 8 4096
    default 128
    help
      How many freed chunks the quarantine can hold (the table is static,
      8 bytes each). A free that finds the table full evicts one chunk
      first, whatever the byte budget says.

config USER_ALLOC_BLOCK_KB
    int "Ring-3 allocator: size-class block, KiB"
    range 8 1024
    default 16
    help
      How much anonymous memory a size class takes from the kernel each
      time it runs out of chunks. The kernel charges a mapping against the
      program's frame budget whole, when it is made, so a small block keeps
      a program with a tight `mem_pages` row inside it; a big one makes
      fewer mmap calls. Rounded up to the page size.

config USER_ALLOC_MAX_MAPS
    int "Ring-3 allocator: live mappings (blocks + large allocations)"
    range 16 4096
    default 256
    help
      Capacity of the table the allocator checks every freed pointer
      against (16 bytes each). A block or large allocation that does not
      fit fails the allocation (null), it is never untracked.

config USER_ALLOC_RANDOMIZE
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: randomize chunk placement"
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      Which free chunk an allocation takes, the order a fresh block is
      handed out in, the position of a large allocation inside its pages
      and the quarantine eviction order come from the kernel entropy pool
      (SYS_ENTROPY_READ_TYPED, so the program's row needs the `entropy`
      capability). Without it the allocator is deterministic: LIFO reuse,
      fresh chunks in address order, large allocations ending exactly at
      their guard page (the tightest overflow detection). A program
      without the capability, or on a boot whose pool is unseeded, runs
      deterministic and prints one line saying so, unless
      USER_ALLOC_SEED_REQUIRED.

config USER_ALLOC_SEED_REQUIRED
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: fault when no kernel entropy is available"
    default y if SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC || SECURITY_CONFIDENTIAL
    default n
    help
      The header checksum is keyed and the placement randomized from one
      read of the entropy pool at the first allocation. With this on, a
      program that cannot get it (no `entropy` capability, unseeded pool)
      takes a recorded fault there instead of running with a key and a
      placement an attacker can predict.

config USER_ALLOC_POISON_FREED
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: poison freed chunks and check the poison"
    default y if !SECURITY_GENERAL
    default n
    help
      A freed chunk's first 512 bytes are filled with a fixed byte and
      checked when it leaves quarantine (or, without quarantine, when it is
      handed out again): a write through a stale pointer is a recorded
      fault, not silent corruption. Cost: a fill and a compare of up to 512
      bytes per free.

config USER_ALLOC_GUARD_PAGES
    bool "Ring-3 allocator: guard pages around blocks and large allocations"
    default y
    help
      Every block and every large allocation is mapped between two
      PROT_NONE reservations (mmap with prot 0: the range is reserved, no
      page is mapped, a touch kills the task). Costs address space and two
      extra mmap calls per mapping, no frames. Off: blocks and large
      allocations are mapped back to back.

# MITIGATION_SPECTRE_V1_INDEX, INFER_MODEL_SIG_REQUIRED and CONSOLE_LOCKDOWN moved
# to the Mitigations menu (config/axes/Kconfig.security), wave 11.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hardware control-flow integrity (plan item I4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One model on every ISA: forward edges (an indirect call or jump may only
# land on a landing pad) and backward edges (a return address is signed or
# kept on a shadow stack). The per-ISA never/probe/require choices already
# exist next to the other ISA extensions (config/arch/Kconfig.isa-aarch64):
#
#   aarch64  A64_BTI (landing pads)        A64_PAUTH (signed returns)
#   x86_64   X86_CET_IBT (ENDBR64)         X86_CET_SHSTK (shadow stack)
#   riscv64  RV_ZICFILP, RV_ZICFISS (probe and refusal only, below)
#   aarch64  also A64_GCS (probe and refusal only, below)
#
# Any policy but `n` makes the kernel AND the user images compile with the
# pads (tools/build/kconfig_to_cargo.py, cfi_rustflags: the pads are architectural
# no-ops on a CPU without the extension); the boot then uses the extension
# when the CPU has it (probe) or refuses the CPU (require). Kconfig is the
# only source: there is no second switch. The aarch64 signed returns are a
# ring-3/kernel boundary only with A64_PAUTH_PROC_KEYS (per-process keys, the
# kernel's key restored on every entry from EL0; A64_PAUTH help).
#
# Not done, and why (the reasons are what a later change starts from):
#   * x86_64 kernel shadow stack (X86_CET_SHSTK): the policy and the CPUID
#     row exist, the kernel does not turn it on. It needs a shadow-stack page
#     set per kernel task and per IST stack, IA32_PL0_SSP switched in
#     `context_switch`, and the retpoline thunk (a `ret` that does not match
#     its `call`) off, i.e. X86_MIT_SPECTRE_V2 not `probe` on a CPU that
#     lacks eIBRS/AutoIBRS. Ring 3 shadow stacks come with the per-process
#     CET state (XSAVES supervisor component).
#   * aarch64 GCS (FEAT_GCS, Armv9.4): probed (A64_GCS, default n). The
#     return-address stack is the aarch64 twin of the shadow stack and needs
#     no compiler mode for the kernel (BL and RET do the work). It needs GCS
#     pages, which stage 1 expresses only as an indirect permission: the
#     kernel's mappings run on FEAT_S1PIE with A64_S1PIE (PIR_EL1 and
#     PIRE0_EL1 give every produced index the meaning the AP/XN bits have,
#     and one index is the kernel GCS page); that excludes the hypervisor
#     stub and the split monitor until they save or allow TCR2_EL1. Next, a
#     GCS per task next to its stack, GCSPR_EL1 in
#     `context_switch`, and every resume that is not a matched BL/RET:
#     arch/aarch64/src/cpu/cfi.rs, "GCS", lists them.
#   * riscv64 Zicfilp (`lpad` at every function entry): RV_ZICFILP probes the
#     device-tree token and refuses a `require` hart, nothing enables it. The
#     compiler half exists (`-Z cf-protection=branch` plus the LLVM feature
#     `experimental-zicfilp`), but the pinned compiler (rustc 1.95 nightly of
#     2026-02-21, LLVM 22.1) lists the feature as unknown and unstable and warns
#     once per crate, which tools/gate/ci_check.sh fails on. Enabling it needs
#     the S-mode enable from M-mode (menvcfg.LPE; SBI FWFT feature LANDING_PAD),
#     `lpad` in the riscv64 entry assembly and the SPELP state in the trap path.
#     Zicfiss (RV_ZICFISS, shadow stack) needs menvcfg.SSE (FWFT SHADOW_STACK),
#     a shadow-stack CSR switched in `context_switch` and `sspush`/`sspopchk`,
#     illegal on a hart without Zimop.
#   * kCFI (`-Z sanitizer=kcfi`, the software forward-edge check for CPUs
#     without BTI/IBT/Zicfilp): the pinned compiler takes it for aarch64
#     and x86_64 and refuses riscv64 ("kcfi sanitizer is not supported for
#     this target"). Not wired: every indirect call into hand-written asm
#     would need the type hash the compiler puts before a function, and the
#     trap needs its own decoder. Without a user it stays a note.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kernel self-protection of the page tables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config PT_PROTECTION_SOFT
    # cargo-feature: pt-protection-soft
    bool "Page-table pool: software write window where the CPU has no supervisor key"
    depends on !HYP_MONITOR
    default n
    help
      On a CPU without a supervisor key (riscv64, aarch64, an x86_64 CPU without
      PKS, or X86_PKS n) the kernel root's page-table pool is mapped read-only in
      the direct map, and `pt_set` writes a word with that one page flipped
      writable for the store, interrupts off, one window at a time (the same
      `ptprot::write` funnel as the key). Where the CPU has the key, the key is
      used and this is not. Cost per kernel-root PTE word: two leaf stores, two
      single-page TLB invalidations (a broadcast on aarch64, plus an SBI remote
      fence at the close on riscv64 SMP) and an interrupt mask, kernel mappings
      only; a user page-table word and a fault pay the range compare of the
      pool. This is bug hardening, not a boundary against an attacker: the
      leaves of the pool's pages sit in ordinary writable tables. On aarch64
      and riscv64 a window's close flushes every CPU's translation of the page;
      on x86_64 SMP the flush is local (a shootdown IPI cannot be awaited with
      interrupts off), so a CPU that read the page during a window may keep a
      writable translation until it is evicted and the protection holds on the
      writing CPU only; the boot line says which. n: nothing built unless a
      hardware key asks for the pool.

config PT_PROTECTION
    # cargo-feature: pt-protection
    bool
    default y if PT_PROTECTION_SOFT
    default y if ARCH_X86_64 && !X86_PKS_NEVER && !HYP_MONITOR
    default y if ARCH_AARCH64 && !A64_POE_NEVER && !HYP_MONITOR
    default n
    help
      The kernel root's page tables come from a pool (PT_POOL_PAGES) the
      kernel keeps write-protected from itself: pool pages carry a supervisor
      protection key that denies writes, and `pt_set` (the one funnel every
      kernel-half PTE write goes through) opens the write for the one store
      and closes it again with interrupts off. A stray kernel store into a word of the kernel
      root's own tables faults. Not covered: a user root's page tables, which
      hold their own copies of the kernel's top-level entries (a store there
      remaps the kernel half while that task runs), and device DMA (the IOMMU). With the split monitor the same funnel is the
      monitor's (stage 2), so this is not built there. Derived, never asked:
      the per-ISA key choice (X86_PKS) is the Kconfig. Cost, where the CPU has
      the key: two WRMSRs per kernel-half PTE word (mapping
      kernel stacks, device windows, block splits); a user page-table word
      and a fault pay one range compare against the pool, the cost the split
      monitor's routing already has. aarch64's key is the stage-1
      permission overlay (A64_POE); riscv64 has no supervisor key hardware
      (PT_PROTECTION_SOFT is its window).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Allocator hardening (audit findings D and E, allocator side)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

config SLAB_HARDENED
    bool "Harden the slab and buddy free lists (obfuscated links, safe unlink)"
    default y
    help
      Slab: the link a freed object keeps in its first word is stored as
      next ^ secret ^ byteswap(address of the word) (Linux SLAB_FREELIST_
      HARDENED), with one secret per slab page. Every pop checks that the
      decoded link is null or the start of an object inside the same slab;
      every free checks that the pointer is an object of the slab and is not
      the one freed last. The list of slabs with room is unlinked only if
      prev->next == self and next->prev == self. Buddy: a block leaves its
      free list only if both neighbours link back to it and its descriptor
      still names the order of the list. Each failure panics with the
      addresses involved (the panic record is the crash-log entry).
      Release builds included: nothing here is a debug_assert.

      On in every build, certifiable ones too: the checks and the secret
      never change which object or block an allocation returns, so they
      cost no determinism. Cost: one xor per link, one range test and one
      remainder per slab pop and free, two loads per buddy unlink.
      Secret source: the entropy pool, drawn with interrupts on when a slab
      page is created and the pool is seeded; before that, or when the page
      is created with interrupts masked, the timer tick mixed with the
      page address. A weak secret still defeats a blind overwrite of a link
      but not an attacker who knows the boot timing; the pool-seeded ones
      are not guessable.

config ALLOC_RANDOM
    bool "Randomize allocation order (slab carve order, buddy list order)"
    default n if BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE || SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC
    default y
    help
      Slab (Linux SLAB_FREELIST_RANDOM): the objects of a new slab page are
      first handed out in a random order (a random start and a random step
      coprime with the object count, drawn per page) instead of ascending
      addresses, so the neighbour of an object cannot be predicted from the
      allocation sequence. Buddy: once the entropy pool has been drawn
      (the slab layer feeds the page allocator a word with every slab page
      it creates), and every block pushed after that goes to the head or
      the tail of its list on a random bit. Each free list is also rotated
      once by a random number of blocks, at boot when the entropy pool is
      seeded (no seed at boot: no rotation). Blocks pushed before the first
      draw (early boot) keep the plain order.

      Default n when BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE or SECURITY_SEPARATION_STATIC
      (the industrial and automotive presets): there the same inputs must
      give the same addresses and the same latency on every boot, and
      randomized placement is the one thing here that breaks it. y
      everywhere else. The seed for the page allocator's placement bits
      rides on slab page creation.
      Cost: a few instructions per slab page created and per buddy push;
      the order of free lists after reclaim differs from boot to boot.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kernel address-space layout randomization (virtual KASLR)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the slide exists: every ISA, except an image that has no MMU to map it
# at another address (riscv64 on a small board, HWCLASS_EMBEDDED, runs in the
# firmware's identity map) and the x86_64 Limine image, which the loader
# places itself (X86_BOOT_LIMINE; its ELF is not position-independent and the
# loader's own KASLR is off for it). A choice hidden by `depends on` reads as
# `probe`, so the Rust side asks this symbol first (azos_arch_api::kaslr).
config KASLR_AVAILABLE
    def_bool ARCH_AARCH64 || (ARCH_RISCV64 && !HWCLASS_EMBEDDED) || (ARCH_X86_64 && !X86_BOOT_LIMINE)

choice
    prompt "Kernel address-space layout randomization (KASLR)"
    depends on KASLR_AVAILABLE
    default KASLR_REQUIRE if BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE || SECURITY_HARDENED
    default KASLR_PROBE
    help
      Where the kernel image sits in virtual memory. Any policy but n
      links the kernel position-independent (a `-pie` link, kernel/build.rs,
      with the code PC-relative, tools/build/kconfig_to_cargo.py kaslr_rustflags;
      a boot that finds no relocation table refuses to slide): the boot
      picks a random base for the image inside the ISA's image window
      (arch/*/src/mmu/kaslr.rs), maps the image there and applies
      the image's relative relocations (`R_*_RELATIVE`) with the distance from
      the link address, before any Rust that needs a pointer runs. A leaked
      kernel pointer then no longer says where the rest of the image is, and a
      fixed-address gadget or a fixed-address data target is gone.

      What moves and what does not: only the image. The map of RAM keeps its
      address (`phys_to_virt` stays one add or one OR: aarch64's linear map,
      x86_64's direct map, riscv64's identity) and the image window does not
      overlap it, so a slid image is a second mapping of its own frames; the
      RAM map's alias of the image is made read-only and not executable at boot
      (kernel/src/boot/early.rs). The direct map's base is not randomized: it
      would turn every `phys_to_virt` into a load of a runtime base.

      | ISA     | window                                   | step   | bits (30 MiB image) | seed                       |
      | aarch64 | the upper half of the TTBR1 range        | 2 MiB* | ~16                 | RNDRRS, device tree        |
      | riscv64 | the Sv39 upper half, 256 GiB             | 2 MiB  | ~16                 | Zkr `seed` CSR, device tree|
      | x86_64  | the first GiB of the top 2 GiB           | 2 MiB  | ~9 (8 whole)        | RDSEED, RDRAND             |

      (*) 2 MiB at a 4 KiB granule; 16 KiB pages slide in 32 MiB steps, 64 KiB
      pages in 512 MiB steps (tests/host/kaslr-tests assert the counts).
      x86_64 is held to the top 2 GiB by `-mcmodel=kernel`, and its AP start
      tables map one GiB of image; Linux has the same 9 bits there. The
      physical place of the image does not move (PVH loads it at 1 MiB).

      The seed is the CPU's source (RNDRRS with A64_RNG, the `seed` CSR with
      RV_ZKR, RDSEED then RDRAND with X86_RDSEED / X86_RDRAND, each unless its
      own choice is n), mixed with the device tree's `/chosen/kaslr-seed` and
      `/chosen/rng-seed` when the loader supplies them (x86_64's PVH start-info
      carries no tree). The boot erases those properties after reading them, as
      Linux does. The seed is what `probe` looks for and `require` insists on.

      n: the link address is the run address, the image is not
      position-independent and the boot code is not built. probe: slide when
      a seed exists, else run at the link address (the boot says so on the
      console). require: refuse to boot without a seed or a slot, before the
      console exists (the PL011 on aarch64, SBI's console on riscv64, COM1 on
      x86_64).

      Cost: the relocation table is part of the image (24 bytes per
      pointer-bearing word: tens of KiB), the boot applies it once (a few
      hundred thousand cycles), and position-independent code loads statics
      through PC-relative addressing as before while function and vtable
      tables become relocated data. Not a cost at run time otherwise, except
      that `virt_to_phys` of an address in the image window takes the image's
      recorded base instead of the one constant (an add and a compare more).

      The default is require for BUILD_TYPE_CERTIFIABLE and for
      SECURITY_HARDENED (an image that runs third-party code, or is certified,
      must not run at a fixed layout by accident), probe everywhere else,
      BUILD_TYPE_DEV included: a development image slides like a release one,
      so a layout bug shows up where it is written, not on the first release
      build. A debugger or a trace that needs fixed addresses sets n by hand
      (the gate's `plain` KASLR rows do, tools/image/kaslr_image.sh). The slide
      itself is printed only on a BUILD_TYPE_DEV image; other builds print that
      the image is slid and by how many bits.

      Not available (the choice is hidden, KASLR_AVAILABLE): riscv64 on a
      small board (HWCLASS_EMBEDDED), whose image runs in the firmware's
      identity map without an MMU of its own, and the x86_64 Limine image,
      which the loader places.

    config KASLR_NEVER
        bool "n: link address = run address"
    config KASLR_PROBE
        bool "probe: slide when the boot finds a seed, else run at the link address"
    config KASLR_REQUIRE
        bool "require: refuse to boot without a seed"
endchoice
